WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.080
Hello I'm Terry, Terry Pierce

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.760
and I've been making telescope mirrors for quite a number of years now, more than forty.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.200
And I run telescope making classes to show people how to do the same thing.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.160
When we're talking about making a mirror,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.700
the opticians hundreds and hundreds of hundreds of years ago,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.080
used different grades of sand which is nowadays called really Emery powder.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.760
So, they would take their metal mirror, they would grind it to curve with the coarse grinding sand.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.060
They would make it smoother and smoother with finer and finer grains of sand,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.480
until they had a very very smooth
surface.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.920
By the time you've finished and you're on the very finest grinding powders

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.600
it's unbelievably smooth, beautiful smooth silky finish to it.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.520
They would then have an absorbent surface like pitch or even paper or silk or taffeta.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.160
So the idea is, you put the pitch onto the grinding tool or a similar circular disc.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.840
That pitch is moulded to match the curve of your mirror that you've ground very finely.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.320
Once that pitch is hard and set you put
your very very fine polishing powder onto your mirror,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:07.840
and you start polishing.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.160
The powder beds into the pitch. It doesn't roll around it just sits there

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.320
and microscopically it acts like a plane and it would make the surface go shiny again.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.360
You are aiming to make an optical surface which is accurate to about a millionth of an inch.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.800
To polish a mirror is at least four, five, six hours of work.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.160
The one thing you must do
is not to keep looking at the clock

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.120
because what you think is ten minutes is
only one minute passed.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.920
There are various mentions of mirror
makers, earlier mirror makers,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.800
even Herschel for example, as far as I can
gather, once he'd got started on

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.680
polishing a mirror he dare not stop and he'd work ten, twelve, sixteen hours without stopping.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.280
With his sister helping and feeding him while he carried on polishing away on his mirror

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.400
to get this absolute perfect optical finish.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.160
A mirror the size of the one we are making here
was the size of the one that Herschel

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:13.570
used to discover the planet Uranus with
and a little disk that size properly

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:18.070
made if you point it at the moon that we
show you craters on the moon no more

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.450
than the mile across, and it will show
you cracks on the moon probably about as

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.920
wide as the River Thames.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:30.040
And all from a little polished piece of piece of metal. Cast, ground and polished by hand.

